From: Francesco Caligiuri <276621@studenti.unimore.it>
Date: Mon, 13 Apr 2026
Subject: [PATCH] fs/fserror: replace igrab() with ihold() to fix softirq lockdep violation

fserror_report() calls igrab(inode), which acquires inode->i_lock via a
plain spin_lock().  This is safe from process context, but fserror_report()
can also be called from a softirq context via the iomap read-completion
path:

  ksoftirqd
    handle_softirqs
      blk_complete_reqs          (BLOCK softirq)
        lo_complete_rq            (loop device)
          blk_mq_end_request
            blk_update_request
              bio_endio
                iomap_read_end_io
                  iomap_finish_folio_read
                    fserror_report
                      igrab         <-- acquires inode->i_lock

lockdep records inode->i_lock (lock class i_lock_key) as SOFTIRQ-ON-W the
first time it is acquired from process context with softirqs enabled (e.g.
during XFS mount via unlock_new_inode -> xfs_iget -> xfs_mountfs).  When
igrab() then acquires the same lock class from a softirq context it flags
the combination as IN-SOFTIRQ-W, producing:

  WARNING: inconsistent lock state
  inconsistent {SOFTIRQ-ON-W} -> {IN-SOFTIRQ-W} usage.
  ksoftirqd/0 takes: (&sb->s_type->i_lock_key#34){+.?.}, at: igrab+0x2a

The theoretical deadlock scenario lockdep warns about:

  CPU0
  ----
  lock(&sb->s_type->i_lock_key#34);  /* process context, BH on */
    <softirq fires>
    lock(&sb->s_type->i_lock_key#34);  /* deadlock */

Fix this by replacing igrab() with ihold().  ihold() increments i_count
atomically without acquiring any spinlock, so it is safe to call from
softirq context.

ihold() is correct here because every caller of fserror_report() that
passes a non-NULL inode must already hold a valid reference to that inode
(e.g. the VFS page-cache code holds a reference via the folio under I/O).
An inode with at least one live reference cannot have the I_FREEING flag
set, so the I_FREEING check performed by igrab() is unnecessary and can
be removed.  The existing NULL-return path (lost_event) is also removed
as ihold() never fails.

Reported-by: syzbot <syzkaller@googlegroups.com>
Link: https://syzkaller.appspot.com/bug?extid=5eb0d61dfb76ca12670c
Fixes: (introduction of fserror_report with igrab)
Signed-off-by: Francesco Caligiuri <276621@studenti.unimore.it>

---
 fs/fserror.c | 19 +++++++++----------
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 10 deletions(-)

diff --git a/fs/fserror.c b/fs/fserror.c
index XXXXXXX..YYYYYYY 100644
--- a/fs/fserror.c
+++ b/fs/fserror.c
@@ -147,20 +147,19 @@ void fserror_report(struct super_block *sb, struct inode *inode,

 	event->type = type;
 	event->pos = pos;
 	event->len = len;
 	event->error = error;

 	/*
 	 * Can't iput from non-sleeping context, so grabbing another reference
 	 * to the inode must be the last thing before submitting the event.
 	 */
 	if (inode) {
-		event->inode = igrab(inode);
-		if (!event->inode)
-			goto lost_event;
+		/*
+		 * Use ihold() instead of igrab() to avoid acquiring inode->i_lock.
+		 * fserror_report() may be called from softirq context (e.g. via
+		 * bio_endio -> iomap_finish_folio_read), but i_lock_key is also
+		 * acquired from process context with softirqs enabled (e.g.
+		 * unlock_new_inode during XFS mount).  This inconsistency triggers
+		 * a lockdep warning for a potential self-deadlock on i_lock.
+		 *
+		 * ihold() is safe here: the caller must hold a valid reference to
+		 * @inode, so it cannot be in the I_FREEING state.
+		 */
+		ihold(inode);
+		event->inode = inode;
 	}

 	/*
